🧩 What is a Set?
A collection of **unique elements** (no duplicates).
Does **not** store key–value pairs.
Looks like: `[A, B, C]`

```java
Set<String> fruits = new HashSet<>();
fruits.add("Apple");
fruits.add("Banana");
fruits.add("Apple"); // duplicate ignored

System.out.println(fruits); // [Apple, Banana] (order not guaranteed)
System.out.println(fruits.contains("Apple")); // true — access by value, not only iteration
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🧩 What is a Map?
A collection of **key–value pairs**.
Keys are unique; values may repeat.
Looks like: `{A=1, B=2}`

```java
Map<String, Integer> scores = new HashMap<>();
scores.put("Ali", 90);
scores.put("Sara", 85);
scores.put("Ali", 95); // replaces previous value for key "Ali"

System.out.println(scores); // {Ali=95, Sara=85}
System.out.println(scores.get("Ali")); // 95
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🔍 Map vs Set – Key Differences

| Feature       | **Set**                               | **Map**                                          |
| ------------- | ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| Stores        | Only values                           | Key–value pairs                                  |
| Duplicates    | ❌ Not allowed                         | Keys unique; values can repeat                   |
| Access        | By element (`contains`) / iteration   | By key (`get(key)`)                              |
| Example Class | `HashSet`, `LinkedHashSet`, `TreeSet` | `HashMap`, `LinkedHashMap`, `TreeMap`            |
| Null (Hash*)  | `HashSet`: one null                   | `HashMap`: one null key + null values            |
| Null (Tree*)  | `TreeSet`: no null                    | `TreeMap`: no null key                           |

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Summary

| Type  | Purpose                                         |
| ----- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| `Set` | Unique values                                   |
| `Map` | Unique keys mapped to values                    |

Note: `HashSet` is backed by a `HashMap` internally (elements stored as keys).

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Want insertion order?

```java
Map<String, Integer> map = new LinkedHashMap<>();
Set<String> set = new LinkedHashSet<>();
```

| Interface | Preserves insertion order | Class            |
| --------- | ------------------------- | ---------------- |
| `Map`     | ✅ Yes                     | `LinkedHashMap`  |
| `Set`     | ✅ Yes                     | `LinkedHashSet`  |

| Collection | Insertion order? | Use instead       |
| ---------- | ---------------- | ----------------- |
| `HashMap`  | ❌ No             | `LinkedHashMap` ✅ |
| `HashSet`  | ❌ No             | `LinkedHashSet` ✅ |
| Need sorted| —                | `TreeMap` / `TreeSet` |

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) Set ≠ Map: Set has no keys; Map has key→value.
2) Set access is not “iteration only” — `contains()` is O(1) average for HashSet.
3) Null rules depend on implementation (Hash* vs Tree*).
4) Duplicate key in Map overwrites; duplicate in Set is ignored.
5) Insertion order ≠ sorted order (Linked* vs Tree*).
